<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sancai">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancai, meaning three colours, is a type of Chinese lead-glazed pottery decorated mainly in amber, green and cream, which flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. It was widely used for tomb figures, including horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty cite sancai wares, together with white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, as products that reached markets across the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancai.jpg" alt="Sancai" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Dobson, photographer employed by National Trust · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greenway_BactrianCamel_AgathaChristie_January2024_NT_CCBYSA_open.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sancai is lead-glazed earthenware with a body of white clay, fired at about 800 degrees Celsius. Lead oxide was the main flux, often mixed with quartz in a proportion of three to one, and metal oxides gave the colours: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> turned green, iron brownish yellow, and less often manganese and cobalt were used, the imported cobalt for a costly blue. Though it needed two firings, it was cheaper to make than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> or celadon and suited to large figures, which were cast in sections in moulds and joined with clay slip.</p><p>Except on the carefully painted offering-trays, the colours were usually applied loosely, splashed or spotted, and ran into each other at their edges. Dealers called such wares egg-and-spinach. The vessels, none very large and found almost only in burials, often borrow shapes from metalwork, including the amphora and the oinochoe with a spout shaped like a bird's or phoenix's head; they may have been cheaper copies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> vessels for the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Green and brown glazes were known under the Han, though rarely mixed in one piece, and then almost disappear for about four hundred years, leaving only a tenuous tradition linking Han to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang. Northern Qi tombs of 550 to 577 have yielded forerunners with splashed green designs. The full three-colour combination appeared shortly before the end of the 7th century and is called Tang sancai; because it was first unearthed and is most often found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, it is also known as Luoyang Tang sancai. After about 70 years the making of tomb figures almost ceased with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> of 755 and a Tibetan invasion of 763, though vessels went on for fifty years or more.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Sancai was made again from the late Tang and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, often for temples, such as the life-size Yixian glazed pottery luohans built around iron bars. Along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> it influenced Syrian, Cypriot and Italian pottery from the 13th to the mid-15th century, and Japanese wares such as Nagayo ware. Qing emperors with antiquarian tastes had it revived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingdezhen">Jingdezhen</a> porcelain. In the 1980s and early 1990s reproductions served as Chinese state gifts to foreign leaders, and more than 3,000 small workshops around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> produced copies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sancai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ceramic technology reached a classical maturity under the Tang, with the white Xing wares, the three-colour (<b>sancai</b>) glazed wares and the painted wares of Changsha, which reached markets across the Islamic world and East Africa and established Chinese ceramics as a prestige commodity in world trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
